  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121903,
      "SummaryText": "It is the policy of Citco Water, Inc.  White House, TN facility management to implement the requirements of this Risk Management Program (RMP) in accordance with the USEPA regulations under 40 CFR Part 68 Risk Management Program Level 3. The objective is to minimize the risk of a release of a hazardous material and if a release occurs, to minimize the potential impact to CITCO Water employees, the public and the environment. This objective will be accomplished by utilizing best management practices, providing appropriate training to all employees, and coordinating response activities, as necessary, with the local emergency response providers. CITCO Water management is committed to providing the resources necessary to implement this policy.\nThe White House, TN facility is governed by a set of USEPA regulations that require planning and facility activities intended to prevent a release of hazardous material, or if a release inadvertently occurs, to minimize the consequences of a release to the employees of the facility, the public and to the environment. These regulations include: 40 CFR Part 68, Accidental Release Prevention 40 CFR Part 302, Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act (EPCRA) The key concepts in CITCO Water release prevention program are employee participation, appropriate maintenance of equipment, and appropriate training of all employees. Employee participation in the release prevention program is encouraged and supported by CITCO Water management. Key personnel are responsible for conducting and implementing the findings from the Process Hazard Analysis (PHA) for chlorine and sulfur dioxide storage. CITCO Water is committed to providing appropriate training to all employees regarding safety procedures. New employees are provided comprehensive safety training during their initial orientation for the facility. In addition, CITCO Water conducts regularly scheduled safety training for all employees each year."
    }
